in this episode of cloud performance  atlas we help a semi-aquatic egg-laying  mammal based calculator unravel its  dependency problem and then we do  nothing stay tuned for more I was  grabbing lunch last week with a good  developer friend of mine and the  conversation turned to lamentations  around the limits of google.com as a  calculator I mean frankly it's one of  the more accessible calculators I've  ever used but I end up hitting the  limits of it pretty quickly and won't  even compute the factorial of 256 my  friend then revealed that she created  her own version of google.com calculator  called platy calc however her version  ran on App Engine and evaluated the  submitted math expressions using the  Python runtime which can easily  calculate the factorial of 256 to me  this was immediately brilliant and I  started using platy calc as my primary  web-based calculator however I started  noticing that the first request of the  day was always a little slower than  subsequent requests seeing as I've been  talking about cold boot time a lot  lately I reached out to my friend and  asked to do some profiling on the  application a first look at platy calc  showed pretty much what I was expecting  higher than normal cold boot time now  the code for this app was really simple  I mean there's no global variables no  locking issues and no NDB fetches in the  startup path the only thing that was  occurring globally during boot time was  that it was importing some complex  numerical processing libraries given the  fact that we've already timed a simple  hello world app and seeing what startup  times look like these imports looked  like a culprit just as the test we  removed those imports and re-time to the  startup process just to see if App  Engine cold boot time had suddenly  regressed or if there was some other  issue going on thankfully we weren't  going crazy as our timing showed pretty  much the same startup behavior as I had  seen before at this point process of  elimination was clearly porting to the  imports as the source of the startup  time issues I mean there was nothing  else going on in the app however it felt  wrong to simply discard them outright we  needed more information as such we put  together a small code snippet the timed  how long it took for each of these  imports to occur  our profiling showed us two clear data  points number one the summation of  imports is easily adding about one point  two five seconds to cold boot time for  the application and number two obviously  some of the import  we're taking longer than others now  number two is really the interesting  point here and it's worth taking a  second to discuss see during cold boot  time your application will be busy  scanning and importing code dependencies  the longer it takes to do this the  longer it will take before your first  line of code is executed now some  languages can optimize this process to  be exceptionally fast other languages  may be slower at it but they provide  more flexibility and to be fair most of  the time a standard application  importing a few modules is less than  impactful however when a third-party  library gets big enough we start to see  them do weird things with import  cymatics they can often mess up your  cold boot time a simple google search  revealed that some other developers saw  similar import time issues so it was  clear that this was the problem we were  running into given the fact that imports  were required for our operation our  hands were a bit tied with respect to an  elegant solution nonetheless we came up  with a few ideas to address the issue  number one use a warm-up request when  the website is loading from static  assets send a quick ping to the  calculator service which will import the  required modules rather than waiting for  the calculate button to be pressed  number to lazy load our imports Python  supports this operation by allowing  global versus local importing of modules  so we could move all of the imports to  be function local so if you just want to  do two plus two you don't have to wait  to import the entire side PI module only  when we detect some specific keyword  we'd import those specific things and  number three prune the dependency tree  we've found a lot of Stack Overflow post  and tutorials about how to manually  prune the dependency tree by either  explicitly importing sub portions orbit  or by doing some fun hacks that included  mirroring the module and removing the  things we didn't need but given all that  we decided to do nothing yep platy calc  is not a huge million QPS service it's  just a small application used by a few  friends who really need to compute large  numbers from a command-line interface  for some crazy reason so waiting an  extra second every few days for a query  that wasn't big of a deal in our case we  decided that's the right thing to do  oh by the way if you're looking for what  the factorial of 256 actually is there  you go  thanks buddy calc now as much as I'd  like to beat the performance drum  is that sometimes the result on the  end-user for a particular perf  improvement just isn't justified given  the work needed to fix it so remember  every millisecond counts except in the  few times where it kind of does
 

